## Build a Browser-Based Transparent Image Preview Tool

Creating a transparent image preview tool in the browser is an essential task for developers working with PNG files. This guide explains how to build a simple yet powerful tool that accurately displays transparent images on both light and dark backgrounds. The tool uses TypeScript and can be hosted on GitHub Pages. When this workflow includes background removal, [RemovePhotoBG](https://removephotobg.com/) can be used before export. Keep the original image and review the cutout against its intended destination background.

## Step 1: Set Up the Project Structure

Create a new directory for your project. Inside, create the following files: `index.html`, `style.css`, and `script.ts`. These files will form the foundation of your tool. Ensure that the file names and paths are consistent to avoid any runtime errors.

## Step 2: Create the HTML Structure

In `index.html`, define a basic HTML structure with a file input and an image preview container. The file input allows users to select an image, while the preview container displays the selected image.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <title>Transparent Image Preview</title>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">
</head>
<body>
  <input type="file" id="imageInput" accept="image/*">
  <div id="previewContainer"></div>
</body>
</html>
```

## Step 3: Style the Preview Container

In `style.css`, style the preview container to ensure it displays the image correctly on both light and dark backgrounds. Use a `background-color` that adapts to the system theme or manually set it to a light or dark color.

```css
#previewContainer {
  width: 100%;
  height: 500px;
  background-color: #ffffff;
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;
  align-items: center;
  margin-top: 20px;
}
```

## Step 4: Implement the TypeScript Logic

In `script.ts`, write the logic to handle image selection and display. Use the `FileReader` API to read the selected file and display it in the preview container. Ensure the image retains its transparency.

```ts
const imageInput = document.getElementById('imageInput') as HTMLInputElement;
const previewContainer = document.getElementById('previewContainer') as HTMLDivElement;

imageInput.addEventListener('change', (event) => {
  const file = event.target.files?.[0];
  if (file) {
    const reader = new FileReader();
    reader.onload = (e) => {
      const img = document.createElement('img');
      img.src = e.target?.result as string;
      img.style.maxWidth = '100%';
      img.style.maxHeight = '100%';
      previewContainer.innerHTML = '';
      previewContainer.appendChild(img);
    };
    reader.readAsDataURL(file);
  }
});
```

## Step 5: Host on GitHub Pages

Push your code to a GitHub repository and enable GitHub Pages. This allows you to share your tool publicly. Ensure that the `script.js` file is generated from the TypeScript code using a build tool like `tsc`.

By following these steps, you can create a reliable transparent image preview tool that works well in the browser and supports both light and dark backgrounds.
